libv4l-rkmpp: make the H.264 encoder reachable from a stateful V4L2 client

Companion to 0001, which got the MJPEG path working. Everything here is
H.264-only; none of it changes the MJPEG stream this board runs on.

The plugin's H.264 encoder works - mpi_enc_test and the plugin's own
Chromium path both produce decodable 1080p - but no stateful V4L2 client
can reach it. ustreamer sets seven controls before it touches a format
and aborts on the first failure, and four of the seven were wrong here.
Measured against ustreamer 6.65 on an OrangePi RK3399.

1. V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_I_PERIOD was refused for any non-zero value.

   V4L2 does separate I-period from GOP size: GOP_SIZE is the IDR
   interval, I_PERIOD the I-frame interval inside an open GOP. The
   distinction only exists for a stream with non-IDR I-frames, which
   VEPU2 cannot produce - every intra frame it emits is an IDR - and MPP
   accordingly has one knob, "rc:gop", for both.

   So refusing I_PERIOD was the more literal reading of the spec, and it
   cost more than it bought. ustreamer sets I_PERIOD and never GOP_SIZE,
   so the refusal made the encoder unreachable rather than approximate.
   Fold it onto the same knob.

   A value of zero is the one case that cannot be folded, and it now
   means "leave it alone" for both controls. V4L2 reads zero as an
   open-ended GOP, which this encoder has no mode for, and passing it
   through breaks more than it approximates: MPP sizes a rate-control
   ring buffer from it, mpp_data_init_v2() rejects size <= 0, and
   bits_model_param_init() then fails with "gop_bits init fail gop_len
   0". kvmd's own default for h264_gop is 0, so this is not a
   hypothetical value.

2. V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_CONSTRAINED_BASELINE was rejected.

   It is not an exotic request: it is the profile WebRTC requires, and
   the one ustreamer asks for unconditionally. Constrained baseline is
   baseline without FMO, ASO and redundant slices, none of which this
   encoder emits, so a stream it produces for "baseline" is already
   conformant constrained baseline. Map both, and map constrained high
   to high for the same reason - it is high without B-frames, and
   B-frames are rejected outright a few cases further down.

3. V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL was stored without conversion.

   V4L2 passes an enumerator, MPP wants the level_idc that goes into the
   SPS, and the two are unrelated numbers: V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_4_0
   is 11, and level_idc 11 is level 1.1. So asking for level 4.0 - which
   is what ustreamer does at 1080p - marked the stream as level 1.1,
   whose limits it exceeds by two orders of magnitude. A decoder that
   sizes its buffers from the SPS gets 99 macroblocks and fails.

   This one is silent: nothing rejects it, the stream just carries a
   level it does not honour. Add the table.

4. V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_REPEAT_SEQ_HEADER was not implemented, and
   implementing it is what breaks the deadlock.

   It is the older spelling of PREPEND_SPSPPS_TO_IDR, which this plugin
   already handles; ustreamer sets the older one. Accepting it is one
   case label. What matters is the consequence.

   With inline headers the out-of-band header is redundant, so
   needs_header goes false - and that is what unblocks a client which
   allocates one buffer per queue, the ordinary V4L2 M2M arrangement and
   what ustreamer does. The separate-header path takes the single CAPTURE
   buffer, fills it with the SPS/PPS, and loops back to the top without
   ever releasing the frame the client queued on OUTPUT. A client that
   dequeues OUTPUT before CAPTURE - the documented order, since the input
   buffer is the one it needs back first - then blocks in VIDIOC_DQBUF
   forever, while the encoder thread waits for a CAPTURE buffer the
   client cannot return because it is blocked. Neither side reports
   anything; the stream simply never starts.

   That path stays for the clients that ask for a separate header. It is
   just no longer what a client gets when it asked for the opposite.

diff --git a/src/libv4l-rkmpp-enc.c b/src/libv4l-rkmpp-enc.c
index 5b6aaae..f6ffadf 100644
--- a/src/libv4l-rkmpp-enc.c
+++ b/src/libv4l-rkmpp-enc.c
@@ -802,7 +802,26 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_streamon(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 			goto err_destroy_mpp;
 		}
 
-		enc->needs_header = true;
+		/*
+		 * An out-of-band header is only needed when the bitstream
+		 * does not carry one. With MPP_ENC_HEADER_MODE_EACH_IDR the
+		 * SPS/PPS arrive in front of every IDR, so there is nothing
+		 * to send separately.
+		 *
+		 * This is also what unblocks a client that asks for inline
+		 * headers and allocates one buffer per queue, which is the
+		 * ordinary V4L2 M2M arrangement and what ustreamer does. The
+		 * separate-header path below consumes the CAPTURE buffer
+		 * before it will look at the OUTPUT queue, and then loops
+		 * without releasing the frame the client queued. A client
+		 * that dequeues OUTPUT before CAPTURE - the documented order,
+		 * since the input buffer is the one it needs back first -
+		 * blocks in VIDIOC_DQBUF forever, while this thread waits for
+		 * a CAPTURE buffer the client cannot return because it is
+		 * blocked. Both sides then sit still with no error anywhere.
+		 */
+		enc->needs_header = (enc->header_mode !=
+				     MPP_ENC_HEADER_MODE_EACH_IDR);
 	} else if (enc->type == VP8) {
 		/* Apply vp8's special configs */
 		if (rkmpp_enc_apply_vp8_cfg(enc) < 0) {
@@ -1019,16 +1038,70 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_h264_convert_profile(int profile)
 {
 	switch (profile) {
 	case V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_BASELINE:
+	/*
+	 * Constrained baseline is baseline minus the three features this
+	 * encoder never emits anyway: no FMO, no ASO, no redundant slices.
+	 * VEPU2 produces a conformant constrained-baseline stream whenever
+	 * it is asked for baseline, so the two map to the same thing here.
+	 * Refusing it is not a safe conservatism either - it is the profile
+	 * WebRTC requires, and the one ustreamer asks for unconditionally.
+	 */
+	case V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_CONSTRAINED_BASELINE:
 		return MPP_H264_PROFILE_BASELINE;
 	case V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_MAIN:
 		return MPP_H264_PROFILE_MAIN;
 	case V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_HIGH:
+	/*
+	 * Constrained high is high without B-frames, which is all this
+	 * encoder can do: B-frames are rejected outright further down.
+	 */
+	case V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_CONSTRAINED_HIGH:
 		return MPP_H264_PROFILE_HIGH;
 	default:
 		return -1;
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * V4L2 passes an enumerator; MPP wants the level_idc that goes in the SPS.
+ * They are unrelated numbers - V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_4_0 is 11, and
+ * level_idc 11 is level 1.1 - so handing the enumerator straight through
+ * marks a 1080p stream as level 1.1, whose limits it exceeds by two orders
+ * of magnitude. Decoders that trust the SPS then size their buffers for
+ * 99 macroblocks and fail.
+ */
+static int rkmpp_enc_h264_convert_level(int level)
+{
+	static const int levels[] = {
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_1_0] = 10,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_1B]  = 9,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_1_1] = 11,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_1_2] = 12,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_1_3] = 13,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_2_0] = 20,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_2_1] = 21,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_2_2] = 22,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_3_0] = 30,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_3_1] = 31,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_3_2] = 32,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_4_0] = 40,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_4_1] = 41,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_4_2] = 42,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_5_0] = 50,
+		[V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL_5_1] = 51,
+	};
+
+	if (level < 0 || level >= (int)ARRAY_SIZE(levels) || !levels[level])
+		return -1;
+
+	/*
+	 * Level 1b is level_idc 11 with constraint_set3_flag, not level_idc
+	 * 9. MPP's h264e_set_cfg() knows that convention and takes 9 for it,
+	 * which is why this table is not simply "level * 10".
+	 */
+	return levels[level];
+}
+
 static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 				 struct v4l2_ext_controls *ext_ctrls)
 {
@@ -1052,12 +1125,6 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 		ctrl = &ext_ctrls->controls[i];
 
 		switch (ctrl->id) {
-		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_I_PERIOD:
-			if (ctrl->value) {
-				LOGE("not supporting I-period\n");
-				RETURN_ERR(EINVAL, -1);
-			}
-			break;
 		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LOOP_FILTER_MODE:
 			if (ctrl->value !=
 			    V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LOOP_FILTER_MODE_ENABLED) {
@@ -1069,6 +1136,13 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_8X8_TRANSFORM:
 			/* Automatically configured */
 			break;
+		/*
+		 * Two spellings of one request. PREPEND_SPSPPS_TO_IDR is the
+		 * H.264-specific control; REPEAT_SEQ_HEADER predates it and
+		 * is what ustreamer sets. Both mean "put the parameter sets
+		 * in front of every IDR", which is MPP_ENC_HEADER_MODE_EACH_IDR.
+		 */
+		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_REPEAT_SEQ_HEADER:
 		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_PREPEND_SPSPPS_TO_IDR:
 			if (ctrl->value)
 				enc->header_mode = MPP_ENC_HEADER_MODE_EACH_IDR;
@@ -1077,6 +1151,16 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 
 			LOGV(1, "header mode: %d\n", ctrl->value);
 
+			/*
+			 * Inline headers make the out-of-band one redundant,
+			 * and emitting both would put a second copy of the
+			 * SPS/PPS in front of the first IDR. See the comment
+			 * in rkmpp_enc_streamon() for why this matters far
+			 * more than a duplicated header.
+			 */
+			enc->needs_header = (enc->header_mode !=
+					     MPP_ENC_HEADER_MODE_EACH_IDR);
+
 			if (ctx->mpp_streaming &&
 			    rkmpp_enc_apply_rc_cfg(enc) < 0) {
 				LOGE("failed to apply header mode\n");
@@ -1176,7 +1260,15 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 			}
 			break;
 		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_LEVEL:
-			enc->h264.level = ctrl->value;
+			enc->h264.level =
+				rkmpp_enc_h264_convert_level(ctrl->value);
+			if (enc->h264.level < 0) {
+				enc->h264.level = 40;
+				LOGE("unsupported h264 level: %d\n",
+				     ctrl->value);
+				RETURN_ERR(EINVAL, -1);
+			}
+
 			LOGV(1, "h264 level: %d\n", enc->h264.level);
 
 			if (ctx->mpp_streaming &&
@@ -1223,7 +1315,46 @@ static int rkmpp_enc_s_ext_ctrls(struct rkmpp_enc_context *enc,
 				RETURN_ERR(errno, -1);
 			}
 			break;
+		/*
+		 * I-period and GOP size are one knob on this encoder.
+		 *
+		 * V4L2 separates them: GOP_SIZE is the IDR interval and
+		 * H264_I_PERIOD is the I-frame interval within an open GOP,
+		 * so they differ only for a stream with non-IDR I-frames.
+		 * VEPU2 has no such mode - every intra frame it produces is
+		 * an IDR - and MPP exposes one control, "rc:gop", for both.
+		 *
+		 * So the two V4L2 controls collapse onto rc:gop. Refusing
+		 * I_PERIOD was the more honest reading of the spec, but it
+		 * cost more than it bought: ustreamer sets only I_PERIOD,
+		 * never GOP_SIZE, and treats a failed control as fatal, so
+		 * the refusal made the encoder unreachable rather than
+		 * merely approximate.
+		 */
+		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_I_PERIOD:
 		case V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_GOP_SIZE:
+			/*
+			 * Zero means "leave it alone", not "zero".
+			 *
+			 * V4L2 reads a GOP/I-period of 0 as an open-ended one -
+			 * only the very first frame is an IDR - and this
+			 * encoder has no such mode. Passing it through is not
+			 * a harmless approximation either: MPP's rate
+			 * controller sizes a ring buffer from it, and
+			 * mpp_data_init_v2() rejects size <= 0, so
+			 * bits_model_param_init() fails with "gop_bits init
+			 * fail gop_len 0" and the encoder comes up without a
+			 * working rate controller.
+			 *
+			 * kvmd's default for h264_gop happens to be 0, which
+			 * is how a value nobody typed reaches us.
+			 */
+			if (!ctrl->value) {
+				LOGV(1, "gop size 0: keeping %d\n",
+				     enc->gop_size);
+				break;
+			}
+
 			enc->gop_size = ctrl->value;
 			LOGV(1, "gop size: %d\n", enc->gop_size);
 
